  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 108 AM MDT Sat May 16 2026\n\nWinds taper off for the most part Sunday night, or at least back off \nfrom their peak spreads. The main H5 closed low will track west to \neast across the CO Rockies heading into Monday morning. This will \nleave dry and windy conditions touring NM while precipitation \nchances will remain north of the NM/CO border for the most part. The \nmain 80-90kt jetmax core will cross northern NM, coupled with a \n~992mb MSLP lee surface low over southeastern CO. This will enhance \nwind potential Monday relative to Sunday, notably over northeastern \nNM where vertical mixing tapping into 50kt H7 winds and a sharpening \nsurface pressure gradient will allow for strong southwesterly wind \ngusts to peak 45 to 55 mph. More widespread peak gusts of 35 to 50 \nmph will be focus elsewhere along and east of the central mountain \nchain. Wind Advisories, and perhaps a High Wind Warning or two, will \nconsidered for these areas as we get closer to Monday. Patchy \nblowing dust producing localized lowered visibility and more \nwidespread hazardous crosswinds on area highways will again be \npresent. Winds will be less further west toward AZ, but still windy. \n\nWinds weaken quickly Monday evening and night as the main H5 closed \nlow opens and ejects northeastward out over the northern Great \nPlains. Northerly winds associated with a cold front backing into \nnortheastern NM advance to ~I-40 before behind shunted back and \nveering out of the southeast. Otherwise, Tuesday sees fairly \npleasant conditions areawide with highs in the 60s in the mountains \nand 70s/80s at middle to lower elevation areas. \n\nThe aforementioned cold front backing into northeastern NM advances \nfurther through all of eastern NM Wednesday, and further west into \nthe Rio Grande Valley and Continental Divide Thursday and Friday. \nAmid weaker flow and winds aloft, this will set the stage for daily \nrounds of afternoon thunderstorm activity mainly along and east of \nthe central mountain chain. Isolated to scattered thunderstorm \nactivity could favor the Rio Grande Valley area as well, especially \nby Thursday and Friday to end the work week.",
